---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About AnalyseSpider — free crawling and SEO tools"
  description="Why AnalyseSpider exists, what its tools process and how the new website is separated from the former domain."
  canonicalPath="/about"
  alternatePath="/de/ueber"
  bodyClass="simple-content"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Home", path: "/" }, { name: "About", path: "/about" }]}
>
  <header class="simple-page-lead">
    <h1>AnalyseSpider helps you understand technical website signals.</h1>
    <p>The site combines small free tools with plain explanations about crawling, indexing, redirects and server logs.</p>
  </header>
  <article class="simple-prose">
    <section>
      <h2>What you can do here</h2>
      <p>You can inspect existing logs, HTTP headers, HTML directives, redirect steps or IP addresses in your browser. The result shows what was directly observed and which questions remain open.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>What AnalyseSpider does not do</h2>
      <p>The site does not crawl arbitrary third-party websites, run security scans or promise indexing. Logs and IP addresses stay in the browser. The response, redirect and robots tools also offer a protected fetch of exactly one public URL.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>The former domain</h2>
      <p>AnalyseSpider was formerly used for log analysis, IP-to-country data and technical website tools. Today's website is a new implementation under new ownership since 2026.</p>
      <p>Former software, copy, logos, databases, downloads, users and customers did not transfer. Historical URLs are restored individually, redirected to a matching successor or retired with a real error status.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Connection to Crawl Foundry</h2>
      <p>AnalyseSpider and Crawl Foundry are operated by Matthias Ramahi. AnalyseSpider remains useful without Crawl Foundry. When a Crawl Foundry link appears, the common operator is named beside it.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="editor">
      <h2>Editorial and technical responsibility</h2>
      <p>Matthias Ramahi writes and technically reviews the current AnalyseSpider guides and maintains the tool interfaces. Each technical article links to the primary documentation used for its claims and states when that source set was last checked.</p>
      <p>Examples and tests are either reproducible fixtures published on this site or are explicitly labelled as not yet run. Drafting tools may assist with structure and language; claims, links, examples and release changes are checked against the implementation and cited sources before publication.</p>
      <p>This describes responsibility for the new site. It does not claim authorship of former AnalyseSpider software, texts, data or branding.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
